  "query_preview": "..(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 524 AM EDT THU OCT 29 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, midlevel ridging will \ndominate over the Southeast, and embedded within flow around the \nridge periphery, a shortwave trough will be initially located over\nOklahoma. This wave will translate northeastward with time, \nworking to flatten the ridge somewhat. Meanwhile, a closed low \nwill be located over the Pacific Northwest, with troughing over \nthe Pacific Coast. At the surface, an area of high pressure will \nsit over the Great Lakes, with a lingering cold front draped \nacross the forecast area. \n\nOn Halloween, this cold front will initially sag south, although \nthis boundary will become less defined with time as a weak surface\nlow develops in the vicinity of western Kentucky by Saturday \nafternoon, a response to the aforementioned shortwave as it \ncontinues to lift to the northeast. Atmospheric moisture should \ntick up around this time, with LREF mean PW values climbing into \nthe 0.8-1 in range, roughly the 80th percentile or higher compared\nto climatology. Even so, this shortwave is expected to dampen \nwith time, and rain appears highly unlikely through Saturday \nevening, with both deterministic and ensemble guidance favoring no\nimpacts from this weak system beyond increasing cloud cover. \nHalloween's highs should range from the upper 60s to low 70s, with\novernight temperatures remaining mild (in the 50s) as extensive \nclouds inhibit cooling. Rain chances do return to the forecast by \nSunday as a more defined shortwave approaches the area, with PoPs \npeaking in the 40-50% range on Sunday evening into the overnight \nhours. Depending on the timing of this wave, highs could be \nsubstantially cooler than currently forecast, particularly if rain\nbegins earlier than anticipated. The 00z LREF shows around a 10 \ndegree spread between the 25th and 75th percentile temperatures on\nSunday afternoon, demonstrating this high uncertainty. Given this\nuncertainty, NBM output has been retained for now, with forecast \nhighs in the upper 60s in most locations, although temperatures \ncould trend warmer with a drier solution or cooler with a wetter \nsolution in subsequent forecasts.\n\nNext week will feature a more active pattern. Although rain should\nexit the area by Monday morning as the shortwave progresses\neastward, energy from the closed low originally over the Pacific\nNorthwest will phase with a secondary system lifting northeast\nfrom the Pacific into the Southwest. The result will be a\nsubstantial trough which will push east with time. Ridging will\nbriefly attempt to build back into the forecast area in the wake\nof the previous shortwave, largely suppressing rain chances on\nMonday, even as cloud cover lingers. By later in the day on\nTuesday, however, rain chances look to tick up yet again as this\nsystem approaches. The exact trajectory of this system remains\nhighly uncertain, with substantial spread in the positioning and\ntiming of this feature. Regardless, with such a dynamic system,\nrain appears likely at some point, even if PoPs are a bit broad\nbrushed due to this uncertainty for now.",
